{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++17 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++17 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++17 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_3>\n#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\nusing ll = long long;\nconst ll MOD = 1000000007;\nconst ll INF = 0x3f3f3f3f3f3f3f3f;\nconst double PI = acos(-1.0);\nconst int MAX_N = 300010;\nchar arr[MAX_N];\nint length;\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n    \n    cin >> (arr + 1);\n    length = strlen(arr + 1);\n    \n    vector<vector<string>> dp(length + 1);\n    dp[0].push_back(\"\");\n    \n    for (int i = 1; i <= length; ++i) {\n        if (i >= 2 && arr[i-1] == 'a' && arr[i] == 't') {\n            for (const auto& s : dp[i-2]) {\n                if (!s.empty() && s.find('@') == string::npos) {\n                    dp[i].push_back(s + '@');\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        if (i >= 3 && arr[i-2] == 'd' && arr[i-1] == 'o' && arr[i] == 't') {\n            for (const auto& s : dp[i-3]) {\n                if (!s.empty()) {\n                    dp[i].push_back(s + '.');\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        for (const auto& s : dp[i-1]) {\n            dp[i].push_back(s + arr[i]);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    vector<string> output;\n    for (const auto& s : dp[length]) {\n        if (s.empty()) continue;\n        if (s.find('@') != string::npos && s.back() != '@' && s.back() != '.') {\n            output.push_back(s);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    sort(output.begin(), output.end());\n    cout << output.front() << '\\n';\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n</your_rewritten_code_3>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n    \n    string s;\n    cin >> s;\n    int n = s.size();\n    \n    // dp[i] = set of possible email prefixes ending at position i\n    vector<vector<string>> dp(n + 1);\n    dp[0].push_back(\"\");\n    \n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        // Option 1: add current character as a letter\n        for (const string& prev : dp[i-1]) {\n            dp[i].push_back(prev + s[i-1]);\n        }\n        \n        // Option 2: check for \"at\" ending at position i (1-indexed: positions i-1, i)\n        if (i >= 2 && s[i-2] == 'a' && s[i-1] == 't') {\n            for (const string& prev : dp[i-2]) {\n                // Only add @ if we haven't added one yet\n                if (prev.find('@') == string::npos) {\n                    dp[i].push_back(prev + '@');\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Option 3: check for \"dot\" ending at position i (1-indexed: positions i-2, i-1, i)\n        if (i >= 3 && s[i-3] == 'd' && s[i-2] == 'o' && s[i-1] == 't') {\n            for (const string& prev : dp[i-3]) {\n                dp[i].push_back(prev + '.');\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Filter valid email addresses\n    vector<string> valid;\n    for (const string& email : dp[n]) {\n        if (email.empty()) continue;\n        // Must contain exactly one @\n        if (count(email.begin(), email.end(), '@') != 1) continue;\n        // Must not start or end with @ or .\n        if (email[0] == '@' || email[0] == '.' || email.back() == '@' || email.back() == '.') continue;\n        valid.push_back(email);\n    }\n    \n    if (valid.empty()) {\n        // Fallback: just output the original string (shouldn't happen with valid input)\n        cout << s << '\\n';\n        return 0;\n    }\n    \n    // Find lexicographically smallest with minimum length\n    int min_len = INT_MAX;\n    for (const string& v : valid) {\n        min_len = min(min_len, (int)v.size());\n    }\n    \n    vector<string> candidates;\n    for (const string& v : valid) {\n        if (v.size() == min_len) {\n            candidates.push_back(v);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    sort(candidates.begin(), candidates.end());\n    cout << candidates[0] << '\\n';\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logp